messages.body_plain:
Hi Ian,

On Monday, September 3, 2012 4:06:02 PM UTC-4, Ian wrote:
> model {
>         real mu[Ndata];
>         real sigma[Ndata];
>         for( r in 1 : Ndata) {
>         y[r] ~ normal(mu[r] , sigma[subj [r] ] );
>         mu[r] <-alpha[ subj [r]]+beta[subj [r] ]*x[r];
>         }

The value(s) of mu need to be defined before using them in the normal likelihood for y. This is a difference in syntax between Stan and BUGS. For the same reason, you need to specify sigma before attempting to use it. Also, you can (re)define mu and sigma as scalars rather than allocate vectors, like

> model {
>   real mu;
>   real sigma;

>
>   for( r in 1 : Ndata) {

>
>     mu <- alpha[ subj [r]]+beta[subj [r] ]*x[r];
>     sigma <- # some function of x maybe?;
>     y[r] ~ normal(mu , sigma );
>   }
>

> When I run it in R, it begins to compile the C++ code and crashes.

In this case, we can see the problem. But in general, if R crashes, it would be good to see as much output as it produces.

Ben

