in random effects models, such as the animal model (see previous post), a goal is often to estimate ("predict") random effects also for individuals without phenotypic records. This is possible via the known relationship/covariance to other individuals with phenotypic records. This is a missing data problem, but unlike the problem in section 6.1 of the manual, there are no observed predictors for the individuals without phenotypic record, so the solution given there does not apply, I think. What I did instead was:

data {
  int<lower=0> N_total; ## total number of individuals
  int<lower=0> N_phen; ## individuals with phenotypes
  real y[N_phen];
  matrix[N_total,N_total] A;
  vector[N_total] mu_u_prior;
}

parameters{
 ...
 vector[N_total] u;
...
}

## likelihood loop runs only over individuals with records
for(i in 1:N_phen){
       y[i] ~ normal(beta0 + u[i], sqrt(sigmasq_e));
     }

The estimates of the individuals without record seem sensible. My question is, is this a correct approach.

Frank

If you want a little more juice, you can exploit the fact that

u' * A_inv * u = u' * ( LA_inv * LA_inv' ) * u = (u' * LA_inv) * (LA_inv' * u) = v' v

where LA_inv is the Cholesky factor of A_inv and v = LA_inv' * u . Thus, the lower triangle of LA_inv' is all zeros. Stan is smart enough to ignore multiplication and addition involving zeros and also knows not to carry through a bunch of constant autodiffs.

So, the blocks of your .stan file would look like

transformed data {
  matrix[N,N] A_inv; A_inv <- inverse(A);
  t_LA_inv <- transpose(cholesky(A_inv));
}

model {
 vector[N] v;
 real sum_of_squares;
 v <- t_LA_inv * u;

 sum_of_squares <- 0.0; // or sum(pow(v, 2)) may / should work
 for(i in 1:N) {
   sum_of_squares <- sum_of_squares + pow(v[i], 2);

 }

 lp__ <- lp__ - 0.5 * N * log(sigmasq_u); // non-constant part of log(det(sigmasq_u * A)^-0.5)

 lp__ <- lp__ - sum_of_squares / (2 * sigmasq_u); // log of kernel of multivariate normal

 // rest of your likelihood and priors
}

Ben

