If you are going to use informative priors, Stan makes you apply them to things in the parameters block only. However, since

p(sigma | ...) \propto abs(det( J[sigma -> gamma] )) * p(gamma | ...)

a specification for p(gamma | ...) commits you to a specification for p(sigma | ...) but you have to adjust the log posterior yourself. In this case, the Jacobian is 1x1, so it is just the partial derivative of gamma with respect to sigma (which I totally messed up in my first attempt at a response). And thus

lp__ <-  lp__ + log(0.5 * pow(sigma, -0.5));

is the correct adjustment.

> Chapter 26 of 1.0.1 reference manual covers this in the most detail. If this were a real model that utilized both sigma and tau for some reason, I would declare tau as the parameter and sigma as the transformed parameter (or as a local parameter within the model block) if you wanted to place an informative prior on gamma.

